  "query_preview": "..\nClear skies with daytime temperatures still slowly warming up\nfor the rest of the day. Our afternoon temperatures will be \nsitting below normal, but a few degrees warmer than yesterday. \n\nThe upper pattern will transition away from a zonal pattern as a\nridge of high pressure starts to build over the Texas to\nColorado over the next three days. This will boost our heights,\nand bumping up our daytime temperatures up by 6 to 8 degrees \nstarting tomorrow. Temperatures will continue to notch another \n1-3 degrees through the first half of the work week. This will \nintroduce pockets of Moderate HeatRisk in the region on Monday \nand Tuesday. \n\nMoisture will start to move into the eastern areas of the\nregion by Tuesday due a southerly-southeasterly flow from the \nridge of high pressure. Ensemble model precipitable water \ncontent show an increase values up to 0.6-0.85 inches along the \nNew Mexico state line. Surface dew points will also come up and \nsit as high as 50-55 degrees in the eastern valleys. Yet, there \nare some wobbliness within the ensemble models on how far the \nmoisture will push into the region due to the ridge placement \nand the uncertainty of the trough placement to the west and \nevolution of the trough. The Euro (58% of members) and Canadian \n(25% of members) ensembles have a lean for more moisture and \nwestern push closer to the Tucson metro. Yet, the GEFs leans \nmore eastern along the state line. For now, the best chances for\nthunderstorms and rain showers will be east of Tucson from \nTuesday to at least Friday.",
